  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">As German as Kafka shows early but real scholarly uptake, with 1 OpenAlex citation and 3,161 recorded downloads from OAPEN indicating sustained reading interest. The book also aligns with the SDG frame of Reduced inequalities, reflecting its relevance to questions of identity, migration, and minority writing.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Lene Rock\u2019s As German as Kafka, published by Leuven University Press in 2019, examines how German-Jewish intellectuals and minority writing helped shape modern debates about cultural identity. The record points to a work situated at the intersection of German literature, identity, psychoanalysis, philosophy, and aesthetics, with a clear conceptual focus on how literary and cultural forms renegotiate belonging.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact is usage: the book has been downloaded 3,161 times through OAPEN, suggesting consistent attention from readers over time rather than a one-off spike. Its open availability likely supports this reach, and the monthly download pattern indicates continuing engagement across the year.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Scholarly uptake is still modest but visible. OpenAlex records one citation, appearing in a 2021 book, which suggests the work has entered academic circulation and is being drawn into related conversations. At this stage, the citation record is best read as an early signal of influence rather than a broad measure of field-wide reception.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Additional indicators reinforce the book\u2019s scholarly and pedagogical visibility. WorldCat and library subject data place it in areas including German literature, migration and immigration, and identity, while the open-access record shows availability in repository channels. Funding attribution to KU Leuven and the peer-review signal from the publisher further indicate a formally supported academic publication with a clear footprint in humanities research.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence portrays a book with meaningful readership and an emerging citation trail, especially around themes of inequality, minority identity, and German literary studies. The record is not yet dense enough to support strong claims of wide citation impact, but it does show a work that is being used, accessed, and incorporated into scholarly discussion.</p>",
